    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;According to the Correspondence Theory of Truth, when is a belief considered true?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;When it corresponds to the way things actually are in reality.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Proponents of the Correspondence Theory generally hold a _____ view of the world.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Realist&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What is a major limitation of the Correspondence Theory regarding abstract fields like mathematics or morality?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;It is difficult to identify independent &#39;facts&#39; or physical entities that correspond to such statements.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;According to the Coherence Theory, the truth of a belief consists in its consistency with _____.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;A person&#39;s entire body of knowledge or belief system.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;The Coherence Theory is typically associated with which metaphysical position?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Idealism.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What is the &#39;hallucination&#39; objection to the Coherence Theory?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;A set of hallucinations can be perfectly coherent while remaining entirely false.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Which theory of truth defines &#39;true&#39; as that which is practically useful or &#39;works&#39;?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Pragmatic Theory of Truth.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What is the &#39;utility&#39; objection to the Pragmatic Theory of Truth?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;A false belief may have beneficial consequences, while a true belief may be harmful.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;The theory that the predicate &#39;is true&#39; is superfluous and adds no meaning to a sentence is called the _____.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Redundancy Theory (or Deflationary Theory).&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;How does Jeremy Sherman distinguish between &#39;Honesty&#39; and &#39;Truth&#39;?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Honesty is expressing opinions accurately; Truth is the accurate representation of reality.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Which theory of truth asserts that there is no single key to truth and that it may be manifested in several ways?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;The Pluralistic Approach.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What is &#39;Eudaimonia&#39; commonly translated as in the context of virtue ethics?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Happiness or flourishing.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Kant distinguishes between &#39;perfect duties&#39; (narrow) and _____ duties (wide/imperfect).&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Imperfect&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;In Kantian ethics, a duty that allows latitude in how it is fulfilled, such as beneficence, is called a(n) _____.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Imperfect duty.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What is the primary goal of &#39;applied ethics&#39;?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;To examine concrete ethical problems in real-life situations (e.g., bioethics, business ethics).&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Which branch of ethics explores the underlying assumptions and origins of moral judgments?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Metaethics.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;The principle that truth is the &#39;ideal limit of inquiry&#39; is a version of which truth theory?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Pragmatism (associated with C.S. Peirce).&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;In virtue ethics, what term refers to the &#39;excellence&#39; or &#39;virtue&#39; of a trait?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;$arete$&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Which 20th-century philosopher coined the term &#39;consequentialism&#39;?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;G. E. M. Anscombe.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;According to the source &#39;An Inquiry into the Nature of Truth&#39;, truth is the ultimate objective of our _____ endeavors.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Epistemic&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Russell argues that &#39;Homer did not exist&#39; is true not because of a corresponding entity, but because of a _____.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Fact (the non-existence of the subject).&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Joachim describes a &#39;significant whole&#39; as one where constituent elements _____.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Reciprocally involve or determine one another.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What is &#39;disquotationalism&#39; in the context of truth?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;The view that &#39;is true&#39; is a device used to remove quotation marks and make generalizations.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;According to the &#39;Rights Approach,&#39; which philosopher is the primary source of the belief that all humans have a right to dignity?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Immanuel Kant.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;In Aristotle&#39;s view, how is moral virtue acquired?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Through habit ($ethos$), repetition, and practice.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;The &#39;Justice Approach&#39; to ethics primarily asks which question?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Which action treats people equally or proportionately?&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Which theory of truth is criticized for being &#39;circular&#39; or &#39;uninformative&#39; regarding the definition of its namesake connection?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Correspondence Theory.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Kant&#39;s &#39;Formula of the Universal Law of Nature&#39; requires testing if a maxim is _____ in a world governed by that law.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Conceivable (and rationally willable).&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What is the &#39;liar paradox&#39;?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;A proposition that is false if it is true, and true if it is false.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Which type of consequentialism only requires that an act is &#39;good enough&#39; rather than the best possible?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Satisficing consequentialism.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;The &#39;Common Good Approach&#39; evaluates actions based on how they serve the _____.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Community as a whole.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;In Stoicism, the virtue of &#39;Temperance&#39; is often described as _____.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Self-mastery or rationality to achieve a peaceful state of mind.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Which theory of truth suggests that &#39;truth&#39; is a collective name for verification-processes?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Pragmatic Theory (William James).&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What is the primary criticism of &#39;Maximizing Consequentialism&#39;?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;It is too demanding, requiring people to do far more than is socially expected.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;According to the source &#39;An Inquiry into the Nature of Truth&#39;, mathematical truths exist only in our _____.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Mind&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Which truth theory relies on the process of &#39;radical interpretation&#39; according to Donald Davidson?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Coherence Theory (modern descendant).&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;A true statement that &#39;speaks of things that are, as they are&#39; is a definition attributed to _____.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Plato (or Socrates).&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What is the &#39;Golden Mean&#39; in Aristotelian ethics?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;The virtuous balance between the vices of excess and deficiency.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;In Kant&#39;s framework, what provides the authority for moral demands?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;The rational will.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Which philosopher emphasized the importance of the &#39;personal will and intention&#39; as seen by an omnipotent God?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Saint Augustine of Hippo.&quot;
    }
